    <sys:String x:Key="diag_hw_analysis_1751">Accelerometer / Gyroscope Sensor Fusion Failure. The orientation data from '{0}' is desynchronized. The screen will fail to auto-rotate when a tablet/2-in-1 device is turned.&#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to reset the Windows Sensor Data Service.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_hw_analysis_1801">Motherboard VRM I2C Telemetry Loss. Communication with the voltage regulation modules (VRMs) for '{0}' failed. Software can no longer monitor CPU voltage or VRM temperatures.&#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to reset the device connection state.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_hw_analysis_generic_conflict">Hardware State Conflict. '{0}' has reported a generalized initialization failure, a power state conflict, or a driver crash. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to execute a deep device power cycle, invalidate the current driver state, and securely reboot the endpoint.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_hw_analysis_generic_bus">Bus Enumeration Error. The active hardware tree in Windows is currently out of sync with the physical connections for '{0}'. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to force a strict hardware tree rescan and force the PnP manager to re-enumerate the device bus.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_hw_analysis_generic_config">Device Configuration Fault. The local machine hive governing '{0}' contains structurally locked or corrupted data parameters. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to forcefully uninstall the ghosted device node, strip its configuration, and cleanly rebuild its registry parameters from scratch.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_hw_analysis_fallback">A hardware fault code ({0}) was detected for '{1}'. The Remediation Engine is standing by to cycle the device.</sys:String>

    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7000">Service Control Manager (SCM) Boot Failure. The '{0}' service failed to start due to an internal execution error. The binary executable attempted to map into memory but encountered a fatal fault before registering its Process ID with the OS kernel. This is often caused by missing DLLs or severe file corruption. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to forcibly rebuild the service registry key and verify the executable signature.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7001">Dependency Chain Cascade Failure. The '{0}' service relies on a parent service that has failed to start. Windows architecture is strictly hierarchical; if the foundation (like the RPC Endpoint Mapper or Network Store) is down, all child services will instantly cascade fail. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to trace the dependency tree and restart the broken parent service.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7002">Load Order Group Dependency Failure. '{0}' requires an entire group of system drivers to initialize before it can start. One or more drivers in this strict load order failed during the kernel boot phase, stalling this service. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Review the System Event Log for failing boot-start drivers (Event 7026).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7003">Missing Dependency. The '{0}' service depends on another service that physically does not exist in the registry. This usually happens when aggressive debloat scripts or improper uninstallers delete core Windows components. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to remove the ghost dependency from the service's registry parameters.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7005">SCM LoadOrderGroup Error. The Service Control Manager encountered an invalid group name while trying to establish the boot sequence for '{0}'. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to restore default Windows LoadOrderGroup definitions.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7006">Registry Write Failure. The Service Control Manager was denied access to update the startup status of '{0}' in the registry. This indicates a severe permission lock, likely caused by aggressive antivirus software protecting the HKLM\SYSTEM hive. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Check antivirus logs for blocked registry modifications.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7008">Circular Dependency Detected. '{0}' is trapped in an infinite loop. Service A depends on Service B, and Service B depends on Service A. The SCM has aborted the startup sequence to prevent a complete system lockup. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to sever the circular dependency chain in the registry.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7009">Service Start Timeout. '{0}' failed to respond to the Service Control Manager within the default 30,000 millisecond (30 second) window. This occurs on heavily bottlenecked CPUs, failing hard drives, or when a service is trapped in an endless initialization loop. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to extend the 'ServicesPipeTimeout' registry key to 60 seconds.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7011">Service Transaction Timeout. The '{0}' service is running, but it took too long to process an incoming transaction request from the OS. The service's background thread is likely deadlocked or starved for RAM. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to gracefully restart the service and clear deadlocked threads.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7016">Invalid State Transition. '{0}' reported an impossible state to the kernel (e.g., transitioning directly from 'Stopped' to 'Paused'). &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: The service executable is malfunctioning. Check for software updates.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7022">Service Hung During Initialization. '{0}' started its boot sequence but became completely unresponsive before reporting a 'Running' state. The OS has suspended the thread to prevent it from consuming 100% CPU. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to kill the suspended process and attempt a cold restart of the service.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7023">Fatal Service Termination. '{0}' terminated unexpectedly and returned a specific Win32 exit code. This is a crash. The service encountered a condition it could not handle and voluntarily shut itself down. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Check the Windows Application Event Log for a corresponding Application Crash (Event 1000).</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7024">Service-Specific Exit Code. '{0}' terminated and returned a custom error code defined by its developer, not the standard Windows kernel. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to analyze the developer-specific crash parameters.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7026">Kernel Driver Load Failure. A boot-start or system-start driver associated with '{0}' failed to map into memory during the early OS boot phase. This is critical. A hardware component or low-level security filter is completely offline. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Review recent driver updates or system hardware changes.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7030">Session 0 Isolation Violation. '{0}' is configured as an 'Interactive Service', attempting to draw a UI directly to the desktop. Modern Windows security (Session 0 Isolation) strictly blocks background services from interacting with the user desktop to prevent shatter attacks. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to revoke the 'Allow service to interact with desktop' permission.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7031">Unexpected Service Crash (First Occurrence). '{0}' crashed abruptly. The Service Control Manager has detected the failure and will attempt to apply the first defined recovery action (usually an auto-restart). &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Monitor system stability. If this repeats, the binary is corrupted.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7032">Unexpected Service Crash (Repeated Occurrence). '{0}' has crashed for a second time. The OS is applying the secondary recovery action. The service is highly unstable and is entering a crash-loop. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to isolate the service and prevent it from crashing the broader svchost.exe pool.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7034">Abnormal Service Termination. '{0}' terminated abruptly without passing through the standard SCM stop controls. The process was either killed by an Administrator in Task Manager, or terminated by an aggressive antivirus. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Verify if this process termination was intentional.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7036">Service State Transition. '{0}' successfully entered the stopped or running state. This is standard operational telemetry logging the normal lifecycle of background daemons. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Routine OS overhead telemetry. No action required.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7040">Startup Type Modification. The boot configuration for '{0}' was changed (e.g., from Automatic to Disabled). This was explicitly triggered by a user, an installer, or a system optimization tool. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Verify that this modification was intended.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7041">Service Account Password Rejection. '{0}' failed to start because the associated user account password in the registry is incorrect or has expired. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Open Services.msc, locate the service, and update the credentials in the 'Log On' tab.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7042">Service Paused. '{0}' was successfully transitioned into a paused state. It remains loaded in RAM but has suspended its background worker threads. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: No action required.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7043">Service Control Rejection. The Service Control Manager sent a command (like Stop or Pause) to '{0}', but the service explicitly rejected it. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: The service may be heavily locked or currently processing uninterruptible I/O.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7045">New Service Installation Detected. A new background service or kernel driver associated with '{0}' was written to the system registry. Attackers often install malicious services to establish persistence. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: If you did not just install new software, run a malware scan immediately.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7050">Logon Failure. '{0}' cannot initialize because the Local Security Authority (LSA) rejected its logon attempt. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Verify the integrity of the local service accounts.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7051">Service Account Disabled. '{0}' attempted to start, but the specific user account it is configured to use has been disabled by an Administrator. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Change the service logon to 'Local System' or 'Network Service'.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7052">Missing 'Logon as a Service' Right. '{0}' cannot start because its account lacks the SeServiceLogonRight privilege in the local group policy. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to re-grant the 'Log on as a service' right to the account.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7053">Generic Service Control Manager (SCM) telemetry or minor transition state logging for '{0}'. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Routine OS overhead telemetry.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7100">A 'Ghosting' state mismatch was detected for '{0}'. The registry dictates this service should be running automatically, but the live Service Control Manager reports it has crashed or stopped. This indicates sudden resource starvation or a failing background module. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to forcibly awaken the service and clear the deadlock.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7101">Process ID (PID) Detachment. The executable for '{0}' is running in RAM, but it has completely detached from the Service Control Manager. Windows has lost the ability to monitor, stop, or restart the service. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to forcefully terminate the orphaned PID and attach a new service host.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7102">Orphaned Handle Detection. '{0}' has crashed, but it left behind open handles to files or registry keys. These locked handles are preventing the service from restarting successfully. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to execute a deep handle release protocol and free the locked resources.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7103">Zombie Process Thread Lock. The primary thread for '{0}' is dead, but a child worker thread refuses to exit. The service is trapped in a 'Zombie' state, consuming RAM but doing no actual work. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to execute a thread-level termination command.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7104">Service Control Manager Desynchronization. The internal SCM database thinks '{0}' is running, but the kernel reports the process does not exist. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to re-sync the SCM database with the active kernel process list.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7105">Memory Mapped Ghost. '{0}' has been uninstalled, but its core DLLs are still mapped into the active memory of another running application. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Restart the PC to clear the memory map, or click 'Fix' to flush the DLL cache.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7106">RPC Endpoint Mapping Ghost. '{0}' is actively listening on a Remote Procedure Call port, but the service itself is supposed to be disabled. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to terminate the rogue RPC listener and secure the port.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7107">Named Pipe Connection Drop. '{0}' lost connection to its local IPC (Inter-Process Communication) named pipe. It is running completely blind. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to restart the service and re-establish the IPC pipeline.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7108">Thread Pool Exhaustion State. '{0}' has generated so many asynchronous background threads that it exhausted its allocated thread pool. The service is ghosting because it cannot process new requests. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to throttle the service priority and reset the thread pool.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7109">GDI / USER Handle Ghosting. '{0}' has leaked thousands of UI handles, hitting the hardcoded OS limit of 10,000. The service can no longer draw windows or render memory. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to gracefully kill the service and clear the UI handle leak.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7110">Minor Ghosting or asynchronous desynchronization detected for '{0}'. The service is experiencing minor latency but remains operational. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: The Remediation Engine will continue monitoring the service state.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7200">⚠️ INTEGRITY COMPROMISE DETECTED. The execution path (ImagePath) for '{0}' does not point to a secure Windows directory (System32/SysWOW64). This usually means aggressive debloat software or malware has hijacked the service route. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Immediate remediation required to prevent rogue code execution.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7201">⚠️ UNSIGNED DLL INJECTION. A third-party dynamic library without a valid digital signature has injected itself into the memory space of '{0}'. This is a common tactic for rootkits or aggressive game anti-cheat engines. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to isolate the process and run an integrity validation check.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7202">⚠️ SECURITY DESCRIPTOR OVERWRITE. The Access Control List (ACL) for '{0}' was maliciously altered. Standard users now have the power to stop, pause, or delete this critical system service. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to restore the default strict Security Descriptor Definition Language (SDDL) strings.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7203">⚠️ SVCHOST HIJACK. '{0}' is configured to run as a shared process inside svchost.exe, but its ServiceDll parameter points to an unauthorized payload. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to quarantine the rogue DLL and restore the native binary path.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7204">⚠️ EXECUTION PREVENTION (DEP) VIOLATION. '{0}' attempted to execute code from a memory region marked as 'Data Only'. This strongly indicates a buffer overflow exploit attempt against the service. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to forcefully terminate the compromised service and enforce strict DEP policies.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7205">⚠️ PRIVILEGE ESCALATION HIJACK. '{0}' normally runs as 'Network Service', but its registry keys were altered to run as 'Local System' (NT AUTHORITY\SYSTEM). &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to immediately demote the service back to its secure, low-privilege account.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7206">⚠️ BINARY CHECKSUM MISMATCH. The physical executable on the disk for '{0}' does not match the cryptographic hash stored in the Windows Catalog. The file has been tampered with. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to trigger an online DISM repair and replace the corrupted binary.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7207">⚠️ DIGITAL SIGNATURE REVOCATION. The security certificate signing '{0}' has been explicitly revoked by Microsoft or the publisher. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to quarantine the service. Do not run revoked software.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7208">⚠️ ROOTKIT CLOAKING DETECTED. '{0}' is attempting to hide its active process from Task Manager and the Windows API using low-level kernel hooks. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: This is a severe security breach. Run an offline Windows Defender scan immediately.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7209">⚠️ FILELESS REGISTRY EXECUTION. '{0}' contains no physical executable. It is entirely composed of malicious PowerShell or JavaScript embedded directly in the registry (RunPE). &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to purge the malicious registry keys and secure the OS.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7210">Minor integrity warning for '{0}'. The service binary is healthy, but an associated configuration key has anomalous permissions. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to normalize service permissions.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7300">The fail-safe recovery protocols for '{0}' have been wiped. Normally, if a service crashes, Windows automatically restarts it. Because these protocols are missing, a single micro-crash will permanently kill this service until a system reboot. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to rebuild the native crash-recovery registry keys.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7301">Auto-Restart Action Disabled. '{0}' crashed, but the SCM is explicitly configured to 'Take No Action' upon failure. Crucial background capabilities are remaining offline. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to configure the service to 'Restart the Service' on First and Second failures.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7302">Crash Count Reset Failure. The failure counter for '{0}' has not reset. The service has crashed so many times that Windows has exhausted its recovery attempts and given up. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to reset the fail-count to zero and analyze why the service is crash-looping.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7303">Recovery Command-Line Execution Deleted. '{0}' is supposed to run a specific script or repair tool when it fails, but the command path is missing or invalid. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Restore the correct recovery script path in the service properties.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7304">Reboot-On-Crash Protocol Bypassed. '{0}' is a critical subsystem (like LSASS or CSRSS) that must trigger a BugCheck (BSOD) if it fails. This protocol has been tampered with. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to restore the critical system boot-flag.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7305">Failure Actions Registry Key Locked. The OS attempted to update the crash-handling logic for '{0}', but the registry key is locked or read-only. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to force permission inheritance on the FailureActions registry node.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7306">Dependent Service Recovery Halt. '{0}' recovered successfully, but it failed to automatically wake up the child services that depend on it. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to execute a cascading wake command across the entire dependency tree.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7307">Minor recovery protocol desynchronization for '{0}'. The service will attempt standard restarts, but advanced logging is disabled. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to restore default OS recovery behaviors.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7400">'{0}' is healthy, but its required dependency chain is broken. A parent service it relies on has been disabled. Windows architecture is strictly hierarchical; if the foundation is disabled, the child services will cascade fail. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to trace and repair the broken dependency hierarchy.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7401">Parent Service Explicitly Disabled. '{0}' cannot start because its primary dependency was manually set to 'Disabled' in the registry. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to revert the parent service startup type to 'Manual' or 'Automatic'.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7402">Grandparent Service Uninstalled. The deep dependency chain for '{0}' is fundamentally broken because a core Windows component (like the RPC mapper) has been removed. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to run a deep DISM restoration and replace missing core drivers.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7403">Tag-Ordered Loading Disrupted. '{0}' attempted to load out of order during boot. It tried to start before its required kernel drivers were initialized in memory. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to rebuild the GroupOrderList in the registry.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7404">Network Location Awareness Dependency Drop. '{0}' requires active network connectivity to start, but the NLA (Network Location Awareness) service is dead or hung. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to restart the NLA service and refresh the network stack.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7405">Cryptographic Dependency Drop. '{0}' requires secure decryption capabilities to boot, but the Cryptographic Services (CryptSvc) foundation is failing. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to repair the Catroot2 folders and restart CryptSvc.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7406">DCOM Server Dependency Failure. '{0}' relies on a COM object that failed to register its class ID during OS initialization. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to forcefully re-register the target CLSIDs.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="diag_scm_analysis_7407">Third-Party Filter Dependency Missing. '{0}' requires a specific antivirus or VPN filter driver to load, but the driver was improperly uninstalled. &#x0A;&#x0A;RECOMMENDATION: Click 'Fix' to remove the orphaned filter dependency from the registry.</sys:String>
